<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-20817] lnet: unique portal traffic for one peer serializes on one match table</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20817</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;A unique portal picks its match table &amp;#8211; and with it the per-CPT&lt;br/&gt;
resource lock that guards it &amp;#8211; by hashing the peer NID alone, in&lt;br/&gt;
lnet_match2mt().  Every buffer posted for one peer and every&lt;br/&gt;
message arriving from that peer therefore take the same lock,&lt;br/&gt;
however many CPTs the node has.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the bulk path: a client posts each bulk buffer against the&lt;br/&gt;
server's NID, so all of one client's bulk attach, match and&lt;br/&gt;
completion work for a given server serializes on a single lock.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Measured on a 192 core node over 0@lo, where every target shares one&lt;br/&gt;
NID: with 128 writers, that one lock accounted for roughly two&lt;br/&gt;
thirds of the busy CPU time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A unique portal accepts only exact-match MEs, so attach and match&lt;br/&gt;
see the same match bits.  Hashing the match bits in as well spreads&lt;br/&gt;
one peer's traffic over every match table, while still sending a&lt;br/&gt;
given buffer and the message that matches it to the same table.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The effect is largest where many targets share a NID &amp;#8211; loopback, or&lt;br/&gt;
a server exporting many OSTs on one NID &amp;#8211; but the serialization is&lt;br/&gt;
not specific to that case.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
